crossfont 0.8.0..0.9.0: the Darwin backend mixes `CGFloat` and `f64`, which differ on 32-bit targets

Static review after core-text@20.0.0..21.0.0 (alacritty 0.17.0 uses crossfont 0.8.1; mrustc on
powerpc-apple-darwin stops at the first type error per crate, so this is the next one). `CGFloat`
is `f32` on 32-bit targets (core-graphics-types src/base.rs), and src/darwin/mod.rs assumes `f64`:

- `Font::metrics`: `ascent`/`descent`/`leading` come from CoreText as `CGFloat` but feed
  `Metrics::line_height: f64` (0.7.0 still widened them with `as f64`);
- `Font::get_glyph`: `bounds.origin.x - f64::from(..) + bounds.size.width` adds `CGFloat` to `f64`,
  and `CGSize::new` / `CGPoint { .. }` take `CGFloat` but get `f64::from(..)`.

The casts are identities on 64-bit targets, so rustc's output there is unchanged.

Big-endian hosts (found on the G5 build of the patched crate, src/darwin/mod.rs:450 `Type mismatch
between Vec<u8> and &_`): the `target_endian = "big"` versions of byte_order.rs `extract_rgba` /
`extract_rgb` take `Vec<u8>` by value while `get_glyph` passes `&rasterized_pixels`, so the crate
never compiled on a big-endian target. They now take `&[u8]` like the little-endian ones. With
`kCGImageAlphaPremultipliedFirst | kCGBitmapByteOrder32Host` the bytes of a pixel are A,R,G,B on a
big-endian host (B,G,R,A on little-endian, which the LE code reorders to R,G,B,A), so the big-endian
`extract_rgba` now also reorders to R,G,B,A instead of returning A,R,G,B; `extract_rgb` drops the
alpha byte as before.

0.8.0, 0.8.1 and 0.9.0 have identical code at all these sites. Not compiled on the G5 beyond
src/darwin/mod.rs:450.

